   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.1. Increased capacity of Member States to ensure respect for, promote and realize fundamental principles and rights at work</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Government, with the support of the European Regional Development and Protection Programme for Lebanon, Jordan and Iraq, achieved the following major results in the fight against child labour in Iraq:
1. Worst forms of child labour amongst Internally Displaced Persons (IDPs), Refugees, and Vulnerable Host Communities in Iraq has now reduced substantially. This was done through establishing Child Labour Monitoring Systems (CLMS) and nine local Child Friendly Learning Spaces where cases of child labour are prevalent. This led to: 
- Over 2,000 children involved in the worst forms of child labour are withdrawn and rehabilitated, and children at risk are prevented from engaging in child labour.
- Over 8,000 vulnerable children and families are better protected through a coherent and effective policy framework.
2. National and local capacities to address the worst forms of child labour are enhanced through capacity building training over 1,000 educators and government officials, including policemen, inspectors, as well as workers&apos; and employers&apos; representatives and practitioners. The ILO also provided support to the Government including in establishing a Child Labour Monitoring System, increasing access to education for children through child friendly learning spaces, providing skills training and income-generating activities to older siblings and caretakers of vulnerable children, and further linking families of vulnerable children to existing cash transfer programmes. Additionally, the ILO supported the improvement of legal and policy frameworks related to child labour and raised awareness through a media campaign on the worst forms of child labour.</narrative>
   </description>
